          {
            "commit": "14d61c60ffa079c5c595a7ea85df9710476b041f",
            "subject": "LU-6132 o2iblnd: release and rebuild an HCA on device removal"
          }
        ],
        "author": {
          "name": "Chris Horn",
          "email": "chorn@ddn.com",
          "date": "2026-10-12 21:50:24.000000000",
          "tz": -300
        },
        "committer": {
          "name": "Chris Horn",
          "email": "chorn@ddn.com",
          "date": "2026-10-13 19:53:56.000000000",
          "tz": -300
        },
        "subject": "LU-6132 o2iblnd: reap the conns of an HCA that does not flush",
        "message": "LU-6132 o2iblnd: reap the conns of an HCA that does not flush\n\nDuring a function reset, irdma refuses each QP state change. Then\nthe QP of a closed conn does not flush. A conn goes only after\nthe HCA completes each posted rx and tx. So the conn stays, and\nkiblnd_hca_remove() waits forever.\n\nOne second into the removal, kiblnd_hca_remove() reaps each\ndisconnected conn on the device. The reap waits until no scheduler\nholds the conn and destroys the QP. A scheduler with a failed poll\nnow clears ibc_scheduled, so a later CQ event queues the conn again.\nThe reap then completes the posted rxs and txs with\nIB_WC_WR_FLUSH_ERR. The QP can go before the conn. So an rx post\nchecks the state under ibc_lock, as a tx post does. The reap skips\nan iWARP conn, because iwcm holds its QP.\nCFS_FAIL_O2IBLND_DROP_FLUSH drops each flush completion.\n\n    Soft-RoCE, 2 VMs, fail_loc=0xf203, rdma link delete under lst\n    without the reap: delete blocked for more than 60 s\n    with the reap:    3 of 3 deletes in 2 s, 54 rx and 4 tx reaped\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial nettype=o2ib testlist=sanity-lnet\nSigned-off-by: Chris Horn <chorn@ddn.com>\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5\nChange-Id: I7db0f0f309c358228906696a1f35e5d423d1a5c3\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
  },
